.TH PYMEDIA 1 "August 2026" "pyMedia" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
pymedia - command-line media processing tool based on FFmpeg
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pymedia
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENTS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pyMedia
is a command-line tool for processing audio and video media using
.BR ffmpeg (1)
and
.BR ffprobe (1).

It provides higher-level commands for common media-processing operations
while handling input validation, media information, output files, and
output-name conflicts.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B encode
Encode or transcode media using a configured media-processing pipeline.

.TP
.B gif
Create an animated GIF from video input.

.TP
.B concat
Concatenate multiple media files into a single output.

.TP
.B split
Split media into multiple segments according to specified split points.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Display help information and exit.

.TP
.B --version
Display the installed pyMedia version and exit.

.SH OUTPUT CONFLICTS
When an output file already exists, pyMedia can handle the conflict according
to the selected output conflict mode.

.TP
.B fail
Abort the operation and report the existing output as an error.

.TP
.B overwrite
Replace the existing output file.

.TP
.B rename
Generate a different output filename instead of replacing the existing file.

.TP
.B skip
Do not generate the output when the destination already exists.

The selected mode is translated into the appropriate FFmpeg behaviour where
applicable.

.SH INPUTS
Commands operate on media files supported by the underlying FFmpeg
installation.

pyMedia uses
.BR ffprobe (1)
to inspect media and obtain information required by processing operations.

.SH OUTPUTS
Output files are generated by FFmpeg.

Output paths may be affected by the configured output conflict mode when a
destination already exists.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B pymedia encode input.mp4 -o output.mp4
Encode a media file to an output file.

.TP
.B pymedia gif input.mp4 -o output.gif
Create an animated GIF from a video.

.TP
.B pymedia concat first.mp4 second.mp4 -o output.mp4
Concatenate multiple media files.

.TP
.B pymedia split input.mp4 ...
Split a media file into multiple segments.

.SH CONFIGURATION
pyMedia stores persistent application configuration in the user's
configuration directory.

On Linux, the default configuration location is:

.nf
~/.config/pymedia/config.toml
.fi

Application-specific persistent resources may be stored under:

.nf
~/.local/share/pymedia/
.fi

Runtime state and logs may be stored under:

.nf
~/.local/state/pymedia/
.fi

.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/pymedia/config.toml
User configuration.

.TP
.I ~/.local/share/pymedia/
Application resources.

.TP
.I ~/.local/state/pymedia/
Application state and logs.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
pyMedia relies on FFmpeg and FFprobe being available to the application.

The exact executable resolution mechanism depends on the installation and
runtime environment.

.SH DEPENDENCIES
.TP
.BR ffmpeg (1)
Media processing backend.

.TP
.BR ffprobe (1)
Media inspection and metadata extraction backend.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The operation completed successfully.

.TP
.B non-zero
The operation failed.

Errors may result from invalid input, incompatible media, an existing output
file according to the selected conflict mode, an FFmpeg execution failure,
or another processing error.

.SH NOTES
pyMedia is a higher-level interface to FFmpeg. It does not replace FFmpeg's
full command-line interface.

For operations requiring functionality not exposed by pyMedia, use FFmpeg
directly.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR ffprobe (1)

